{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"6a55380d-7aa5-43b5-b5da-dab003d44df7","title":"A system of two linear equations as crossing lines"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"6a55380d-7aa5-43b5-b5da-dab003d44df7","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Each equation draws a line. A point on a line gives an x and y that make its equation true.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Two equations, one solution"},{"op":"equation","id":"eq1","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"y=x+1"},{"op":"figure","id":"graph","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-1,5],"y":[-1,6],"axes":"edge","x_label":"x","y_label":"y"},"marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"line1","y":"x+1","domain":[-1,5],"label":"y = x + 1"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"6a55380d-7aa5-43b5-b5da-dab003d44df7","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Now draw the second equation. Solving the system means finding a point that lies on both lines at once.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"eq2","anchor":{"el":"eq1","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"y=-x+5"},{"op":"figure","id":"second","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"line2","y":"-x+5","domain":[-1,5],"label":"y = −x + 5"},{"kind":"point","id":"cross","at":{"hit":["line1","line2"]},"label":"(2, 3)"}]}],"checkpoint":{"question":"Why must the solution lie at the crossing rather than elsewhere on either line?","expected_gist":"The crossing satisfies both equations at once."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"6a55380d-7aa5-43b5-b5da-dab003d44df7","step":{"id":"s3","script":"At the crossing, both expressions give the same y. Set them equal: x is two, and then y is three.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"solve","anchor":{"el":"eq2","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"x+1=-x+5\\;\\Rightarrow\\;2x=4\\;\\Rightarrow\\;x=2"},{"op":"equation","id":"gety","anchor":{"el":"solve","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"y=2+1=3"},{"op":"figure","id":"guides","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"cross"},"to":[2,0],"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"cross"},"to":[0,3],"stroke":"dashed"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"6a55380d-7aa5-43b5-b5da-dab003d44df7","step":{"id":"s4","script":"Check the same ordered pair in both equations. Both are true, so the crossing represents the system’s single solution.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"check","anchor":{"el":"gety","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"3=2+1\\qquad 3=-2+5"},{"op":"equation","id":"answer","anchor":{"el":"check","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\boxed{(x,y)=(2,3)}"},{"op":"figure","id":"highlight","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"circle","center":{"at":"cross"},"r":0.22},{"kind":"text","at":[3.15,3.35],"text":"both true","size":"small"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
